    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an expanded explanation of mitosis, focusing on the different stages and mechanics:\n\nMitosis is the process by which a cell makes an exact copy of itself, resulting in two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. This process is crucial for growth, repair, and development in living organisms. Mitosis is typically divided into several stages: interphase, prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase.\n\nIn interphase, a cell grows and replicates its DNA, producing two identical copies of each chromosome. This genetic material is then condensed and prepared for division. The process of interphase also allows the chromatin to organize into distinct regions, or chromosomes, which will be coiled and condensed during cell division.\n\nDuring prophase, the chromosomes condense, become visible, and become \"anchored\" to the cell's nuclear envelope. The nuclear envelope ruptures, releasing the chromosomes into the cytoplasm. Then, in metaphase, the chromosomes line up at the center of the cell, attached to the spindle fibers, which are extending from the centrioles. This alignment, known as the metaphase plate, ensures that each daughter cell will receive an identical set of chromosomes.\n\nIn anaphase",
      "source": "target",
